      "flux": "#!/bin/bash\n\n# Flux directives translated from SLURM\n#FLUX: -N 2\n#FLUX: -n 16\n#FLUX: -g 8\n\n#\n# --- Omitted SLURM options and explanations ---\n#\n# Task and GPU requests:\n#   The original SLURM script requested 2 nodes but did not specify the number of\n#   tasks or GPUs. The script body, however, uses 'mpirun -np 16' and profiles\n#   GPU usage ('hpcrun ... -e gpu=nvidia'). This translation infers the user's\n#   intent was to run 16 total tasks. It also infers a request for 8 total GPUs,\n#   assuming a common configuration of 4 GPUs per node.\n#   Performance implication: If this inference is incorrect, the job may not run\n#   as intended or may use resources inefficiently.\n#\n# mpirun -npersocket\n#   The OpenMPI-specific option '-npersocket 4' is omitted. This is a process\n#   mapping hint. Flux has its own mechanisms for process mapping, and this\n#   option does not directly translate.\n#   Performance implication: The default process layout provided by Flux may be\n#   sub-optimal compared to the original request, which could affect communication\n#   performance.\n#\n# Time, Memory, Queue:\n#   The original SLURM script did not request a time limit, memory, or a specific\n#   queue. These have been omitted. The job will run with system defaults, which\n#   could lead to it being killed prematurely or failing with out-of-memory errors.\n#\nsource ~/init.sh\nmake clean && make\nrm -r hpctoolkit*\n\n# 'mpirun' is replaced by 'flux run'. Flux will automatically launch the 16 tasks\n# requested in the job directives across the 2 allocated nodes.\nflux run hpcrun -e CPUTIME -e IO -e gpu=nvidia -t ./jacobi\n\nhpcstruct --gpucfg yes hpctoolkit*\nhpcstruct --gpucfg yes jacobi\nhpcprof -S jacobi.hpcstruct -I jacobi.cpp -I jacobi_kernels.cu -I ./+ hpctoolkit*\necho \"===DONE===\"\n",
